One piece of feedback about vite-node is that it's not clear how to implement HMR when running the viteNodeServer and the runner in different contexts.

[The current implementation of HMR in vite-node cli](https://github.com/vitest-dev/vitest/blob/db22c6772519f62a6435a9cac21017d4b56441aa/packages/vite-node/src/cli.ts#L88) showcases this
```
const runner = new ViteNodeRunner({
    root: server.config.root,
    base: server.config.base,
    fetchModule(id) {
      return node.fetchModule(id)
    },
    resolveId(id, importer) {
      return node.resolveId(id, importer)
    },
    createHotContext(runner, url) {
      return createHotContext(runner, server.emitter, files, url)
    },
  })

```
`server.emitter` wouldn't be accessible to the runner.

Hi all! Very excited for server HMR and support for different runtimes. Forgive what may be a dumb question — I am a bear of little brain — but how would we go about using this?

If I use the example quickstart code inside a `configureServer` hook...

```js
const server = await createServer();
const runtime = createViteRuntime(server);
```

...it loops infinitely. If I pass in a resolved config instead...

```js
const server = await createServer(vite_config);
const runtime = createViteRuntime(server);
```

...it complains that 'build.terserOptions is specified' (they're not, in the pre-resolved config, but presumably they are part of the resolved config).

Halp! And thanks!

What about this option?

```ts
{
  // node calls "process.setSourceMapsEnabled" - throws an error if not available
  sourcemapInterceptor: false | 'node' | 'prepareStackTrace'
  // or we can also except a function that we export from another entry point
}
```

The default is `node` if the API is supported, and `prepareStackTrace` if not. The question is should we support source map in externalized packages? If not, then the implementation will be very short.
